---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Zero Turn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-zero-turn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Riding Lawn Mower",
    "url": "/best-riding-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Snow Blower",
    "url": "/best-snow-blower/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Compact Tractors in 2026: Sub-Compact to Compact, Ranked";
const description =
  "Six compact and sub-compact tractors compared on weight, loader capacity, hitch category and real equipped price \u2014 plus why the dealer matters more than the badge.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "kubota-bx23s",
    "name": "Kubota BX23S Sub-Compact TLB",
    "category": "Sub-Compact, 21.6 HP, Loader + Backhoe",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 900,
    "pros": [
      "Factory loader and backhoe on a machine small enough to drive through a standard gate and across a lawn",
      "The backhoe is a real one \u2014 we dug a 6-foot trench for a water line without renting anything",
      "Turf tires and low weight mean it works on a maintained lawn without leaving ruts",
      "Kubota's dealer network is dense, and parts availability is the best in the sub-compact class",
      "Holds resale value better than anything else in this test \u2014 often 70 percent after five years"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "21.6 HP runs out of capability with heavy ground-engaging implements like a box blade in hard clay",
      "The backhoe adds weight that makes loader work on soft ground trickier than it looks"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most useful machine per dollar for a 2 to 10-acre property. The backhoe alone replaces enough rental days to matter.",
    "description": "The BX23S is the machine that defines the sub-compact category, and after using one across a season of real property work it is easy to see why it outsells its competitors. The combination that matters is a factory loader and a factory backhoe on a tractor small and light enough to drive across a maintained lawn without wrecking it \u2014 that dual capability is what turns a tractor from a mowing machine into the thing you use for every project on the property. The backhoe is the part people underestimate. It is not a toy attachment: we dug a six-foot trench for a water line, pulled several stumps, and cut a drainage swale, all jobs that would otherwise have meant renting a mini excavator at $350 a day plus transport. Three or four such jobs a year makes a meaningful dent in the machine's cost. On turf tires the BX23S is genuinely lawn-friendly, which matters if the tractor has to cross the yard to get anywhere. The honest limitation is power. At 21.6 horsepower, ground-engaging work in hard soil is slow \u2014 a box blade in dry clay will stall forward progress in a way a 25 or 35 horsepower machine will not \u2014 and the backhoe's weight on the rear makes loader work on soft ground require more care than a lighter configuration. For property maintenance rather than land clearing, it is the most capable small machine you can buy, and the resale figures suggest owners agree."
  },
  {
    "id": "john-deere-1025r",
    "name": "John Deere 1025R",
    "category": "Sub-Compact, 23.9 HP, Loader",
    "badge": "Best Dealer Support",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1400,
    "pros": [
      "The densest dealer and service network in North America \u2014 parts are rarely more than a short drive away",
      "AutoConnect mid-mount mower deck genuinely attaches in under a minute without tools",
      "Excellent ergonomics and visibility; the easiest machine here for a first-time tractor owner",
      "Strong resale value, second only to Kubota in our market survey",
      "Quick-attach loader and a well-designed hood for daily fluid checks"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Priced at a premium against comparable machines from Kioti, LS, and Massey Ferguson",
      "Backhoe is an add-on rather than an integrated design, and it is expensive"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Buy it for the dealer network and the mower deck system. You pay more per horsepower and get support that will still be there in year twelve.",
    "description": "If you intend to mow with your tractor regularly, the 1025R's AutoConnect deck is a bigger deal than any specification on the sheet. Attaching and removing a mid-mount mower deck is normally a twenty-minute job involving lying on the ground, and it is why most tractor owners end up leaving the deck on or never using it. AutoConnect genuinely takes under a minute, driving on and locking without tools, and that changes how often the machine actually gets used for two different jobs in one afternoon. The broader argument is service. A tractor is a twenty-year machine with hydraulic components, proprietary filters, and electronics, and John Deere's network is denser than anyone else's in North America \u2014 in most of the country you are within an easy drive of a dealer who stocks what you need. For a buyer who does not want their equipment ownership to become a research project, that is worth real money. Ergonomics are the other quiet strength: visibility, control placement, and the general approachability of the machine make it the easiest tractor here for someone who has never owned one. The cost of all this is the price per horsepower, which is clearly the highest in this test, and a backhoe option that is both an add-on rather than an integrated design and genuinely expensive. If the backhoe is central to your plans, the Kubota is the better buy; if mowing and loader work dominate, this is the machine."
  },
  {
    "id": "kioti-cs2520",
    "name": "Kioti CS2520",
    "category": "Sub-Compact, 24.5 HP, Loader",
    "badge": "Best Warranty",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 500,
    "pros": [
      "Six-year powertrain warranty is the longest in the category by a wide margin",
      "More horsepower and loader lift capacity than the Deere and Kubota at a lower price",
      "Daedong builds its own engines and transmissions rather than sourcing them, which shows in refinement",
      "Comfortable operator platform with well-placed hydrostatic pedals for long sessions",
      "Generous standard equipment \u2014 you are not nickel-and-dimed on the quote"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Dealer network is genuinely thinner; check for a local dealer before you even consider it",
      "Resale is weaker than the major brands, which erodes part of the purchase savings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "More tractor for the money than anything else here, backed by the best warranty. Only buy it if there is a dealer within a sensible drive.",
    "description": "Kioti has spent two decades turning from a budget alternative into a machine that genuinely competes on capability, and the CS2520 makes the case plainly: more horsepower than the John Deere 1025R, more loader lift than the Kubota BX23S, and a six-year powertrain warranty against the two-year coverage that is standard in the class \u2014 for less money than either. Daedong builds its own engines and transmissions rather than buying them in, and the refinement shows in how the hydrostatic transmission behaves under load and how little the machine vibrates through the platform. The operator station is comfortable in the specific ways that matter over a long session: pedal geometry that does not tire your ankle, controls that fall to hand, and a seat that is properly sprung. Standard equipment is generous too, so the quote you are given is closer to the price you pay than with some competitors. The caveat is distribution, and it is not a small one. Tractor ownership means filters, hydraulic fluid, a hose that eventually blows, and occasionally a warranty claim \u2014 and a six-year warranty is worth nothing if the nearest authorised dealer is two hours away. Check that first, before the specification comparison. Resale is the other cost: Kioti machines trade at a discount to Deere and Kubota, which returns some of the purchase price advantage when you sell."
  },
  {
    "id": "kubota-l2502",
    "name": "Kubota L2502",
    "category": "Compact, 24.8 HP, Loader",
    "badge": "Best for Real Acreage",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 700,
    "pros": [
      "Substantially heavier than any sub-compact here, which is what makes ground-engaging implements work",
      "Category 1 three-point hitch opens up the full standard implement market rather than limited sub-compact sizes",
      "Live PTO and higher hydraulic flow drive rotary cutters and post hole diggers properly",
      "Proven L-series platform with an enormous installed base and excellent parts support",
      "Optional 4WD and R4 industrial tires handle mud and slope far better than turf-shod sub-compacts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Too heavy and too wide for delicate lawn work \u2014 it will mark a maintained yard",
      "A meaningful step up in price, and implements at Category 1 size cost more too"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right machine once you are managing land rather than maintaining a yard. Weight is the specification that matters, and this has it.",
    "description": "There is a clear line in this category, and it is not horsepower \u2014 it is weight. A sub-compact tractor pulling a box blade through hard ground simply skips over the surface, because it lacks the mass to keep the implement in the dirt. The L2502 crosses that line. At roughly twice the weight of a BX-series machine with similar rated horsepower, it does ground-engaging work that a sub-compact cannot, and anyone planning to grade a driveway, maintain trails, or brush-hog pasture will feel the difference immediately. The Category 1 three-point hitch is the second structural advantage. Sub-compacts use a limited hitch that restricts you to a smaller and more expensive implement market; Category 1 is the standard the entire agricultural implement industry is built around, which means more choice, better used availability, and lower prices. Higher hydraulic flow and a live PTO drive rotary cutters and post hole diggers at the speeds they were designed for rather than bogging them. The costs of stepping up are real. This machine is wide and heavy enough that it will mark a maintained lawn, so if the tractor has to cross the yard regularly the sub-compacts are better neighbours. And the price step is meaningful \u2014 both for the machine and for the larger implements it takes. For maintaining ten acres or more, though, it is the honest answer."
  },
  {
    "id": "massey-ferguson-gc1725m",
    "name": "Massey Ferguson GC1725M",
    "category": "Sub-Compact, 24.8 HP, Loader",
    "badge": "Best Hydrostatic Feel",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 400,
    "pros": [
      "The smoothest hydrostatic transmission in this test \u2014 precise, progressive, and easy at low speed",
      "Excellent loader control feel, which is what makes finish grading with a bucket possible",
      "Iseki-built drivetrain with a long-standing reputation for durability",
      "Competitive pricing that consistently undercuts the Deere and Kubota equivalents",
      "Cruise control and comfortable operator platform for long mowing sessions"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Dealer coverage varies considerably by region \u2014 excellent in farm country, thin in suburban markets",
      "Fewer factory attachment options than the Kubota and Deere ecosystems"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The machine to buy if precision matters \u2014 loader feel and low-speed control are best in class. Check local dealer coverage before committing.",
    "description": "Anyone who has tried to finish-grade a driveway with a loader bucket knows that the specification that matters is not lift capacity but control: how finely you can modulate speed and bucket angle at a crawl. The GC1725M is the best machine in this test on that measure. Its hydrostatic transmission is noticeably more progressive than its competitors', and paired with well-damped loader controls it makes low-speed precision work genuinely pleasant instead of a series of lurches. The drivetrain is Iseki-built, a lineage with a long and good durability record, and the machine feels tight and properly assembled in a way that supports it. Cruise control and a comfortable platform make long mowing sessions less of an ordeal than the hours suggest, and the pricing consistently undercuts the equivalent John Deere and Kubota by enough to fund a useful implement. The considerations are commercial rather than mechanical. Massey Ferguson's dealer coverage is strong in agricultural regions and noticeably thin in suburban markets, so the same warning applies here as to Kioti: confirm there is a dealer you can actually reach before the specification sheet seduces you. And the factory attachment catalogue is narrower than Kubota's or Deere's, which matters if you plan to buy several implements from the manufacturer rather than shopping the aftermarket."
  },
  {
    "id": "ls-mt225s",
    "name": "LS MT225S",
    "category": "Sub-Compact, 24.7 HP, Loader",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 600,
    "pros": [
      "Consistently the lowest price per horsepower of any machine in this comparison",
      "Loader lift capacity is strong for the class and the loader is quick-attach as standard",
      "LS builds its own transmissions and has supplied major brands for years, so the mechanicals are proven",
      "Six-year powertrain warranty matches the best in the category",
      "Comes well equipped rather than stripped, so fewer costly options are needed"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The thinnest dealer network here, and support quality varies significantly between dealers",
      "Resale value is the weakest in this test, which offsets a good part of the initial saving"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The most tractor for the least money, if a good LS dealer is nearby and you intend to keep it rather than trade it.",
    "description": "LS is not an unknown quantity dressed up as a tractor brand \u2014 the company has built transmissions and components for better-known manufacturers for years, and the MT225S reflects that engineering background rather than a badge-engineered import. What it offers is straightforward: the lowest price per horsepower here, a strong quick-attach loader, and a six-year powertrain warranty that matches the best in the category, on a machine that comes well equipped rather than stripped to hit a headline price. In use, it does the work. Loader capacity is competitive, the hydrostatic transmission behaves properly, and nothing about the machine feels like a compromise while you are operating it. For a buyer who needs loader work, light grading, and mowing on a few acres, and who is not going to trade the machine in three years, the value case is genuinely strong. The two weaknesses are both about what happens after the sale. The dealer network is the thinnest in this comparison and the quality of individual dealers varies a great deal, which matters enormously on a machine you will own for fifteen years. And resale is the weakest here \u2014 LS tractors trade at a real discount used, so if your ownership plan involves trading up in a few years, a meaningful portion of the money you saved comes back out at that point. Buy it to keep it."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Compact Tractors",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-compact-tractor/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best compact tractor in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Kubota BX23S is our overall pick for most property owners because a factory loader and backhoe on a lawn-friendly machine covers more real jobs than anything else in the class. The John Deere 1025R wins on dealer support and its one-minute AutoConnect mower deck. For genuine ground-engaging work on ten or more acres, step up to the heavier Kubota L2502."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many acres do you need to justify a tractor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Roughly two to three acres is the practical floor, and even then only if you regularly move material \u2014 gravel, mulch, soil, firewood, snow. Under that, a zero turn mower plus occasional equipment rental costs far less and does the job better. The loader, not the mower deck, is what actually justifies the purchase."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Sub-compact or compact tractor: which should I buy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy sub-compact if your work is loader-led and the machine has to cross a maintained lawn. Buy compact if your work is implement-led \u2014 box blade, rotary cutter, tiller \u2014 because weight rather than horsepower is what keeps ground-engaging implements in the dirt. A compact tractor weighs roughly twice as much at similar rated horsepower."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a compact tractor cost with attachments?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect $16,000 to $26,000 for a tractor with a loader, then $2,000 to $3,500 for a mid-mount mower deck, $700 to $2,200 for a box blade or land plane, and $400 to $1,200 for ballast. A backhoe, where offered, adds $5,000 to $9,000. A realistically equipped machine lands between $22,000 and $35,000."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a backhoe attachment worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you have trenching, stump removal, or drainage work ahead of you, yes. A mini excavator rents for around $350 a day plus transport, so three or four project days a year makes a real dent in the cost, and owning one means you do the job when it suits you. If your work is mowing and material handling, skip it and put the money into implements."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which compact tractor brand holds its value best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Kubota, closely followed by John Deere \u2014 both commonly retain 60 to 70 percent of purchase price after five years, which substantially lowers real cost of ownership. Kioti, LS, and Massey Ferguson trade at a larger discount used, so their lower purchase price is partly recovered by the market when you sell. Buy a value brand to keep it, not to trade it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need 4WD on a compact tractor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, in nearly every case. Four-wheel drive transforms loader performance, because a loaded bucket lifts weight off the rear wheels and a two-wheel-drive machine simply spins. It also matters on any slope, in mud, and for snow work. The premium is a few thousand dollars and it is the last option to cut from a build."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Home Fix Reviews" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best compact tractor",
      "sub-compact tractor",
      "Kubota BX23S",
      "John Deere 1025R",
      "compact tractor cost",
      "tractor buying guide"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Compact Tractors</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six machines in the 21 to 25 horsepower class, compared on the specifications that decide what a tractor can really do — operating weight, hitch category, PTO output, and the equipped price you actually pay.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#kubota-bx23s" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            What Size Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-tractor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. When to Buy One</a>
          <a href="#classes-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Sub-Compact vs Compact</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Guide</a>
          <a href="#attachments" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Attachments</a>
          <a href="#real-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. What It Really Costs</a>
          <a href="#dealer-reality" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Dealer Reality</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance &amp; Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A compact tractor equipped the way you will actually use it costs between $22,000 and $35,000, and the specification buyers fixate on — horsepower — is close to the least predictive number on the sheet. Weight, hitch category, PTO output and the quality of your local dealer decide whether the machine does the work. Here is how six popular models compare on all four.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="why-tractor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. When a Tractor Is Actually the Right Purchase
          </h2>

          <p>
            A compact tractor is a $20,000 to $35,000 decision once you have added a loader and two implements, which makes it one of the largest single purchases a property owner makes outside the house and the vehicles. It earns that money in exactly one situation: when you own enough land that moving material is a recurring task rather than an occasional one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest test is the loader. If you find yourself moving gravel, mulch, firewood, soil, snow, or debris more than a handful of times a year, a front-end loader changes the property from something you maintain by hand to something you manage. Everything else the tractor does — mowing, grading, tilling, post holes, brush cutting — is important, but it is the loader that people actually use every week.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
