        <div id="attachments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Attachments: Where the Rest of the Money Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nobody buys a bare tractor. The realistic budget is the machine plus a loader plus two or three implements, and the implements routinely add 30 to 50 percent to the total. Planning that from the start prevents the common outcome of a capable tractor with nothing to hook to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The loader</strong> is not optional. Order it with the tractor — a factory loader is engineered for the machine and adding one later costs considerably more. Look at lift capacity at full height rather than the headline figure, because that is the number that decides whether you can load a truck.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A box blade or land plane</strong> is the first implement most owners buy, and it is what maintains a gravel driveway. A land plane is the better tool for regrading and smoothing; a box blade is better for moving material and cutting. If you have a long gravel drive, the land plane will get used more.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rear ballast</strong> is the most neglected purchase in tractor ownership and the most important for safety. Lifting a heavy load on the loader without ballast lightens the rear wheels, which costs you steering and, in the worst case, tips the machine forward. A ballast box or filled rear tires is not an accessory; it is part of using the loader safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A quick-attach plate</strong> on the loader, ideally the universal skid-steer pattern, opens up an enormous aftermarket of pallet forks, grapples, and buckets at reasonable prices. A proprietary attachment pattern locks you into one manufacturer's catalogue at their prices for the life of the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

