        <div id="dealer-reality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Dealer Matters More Than the Brand
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single piece of advice most likely to be ignored and most likely to be regretted. A tractor is a twenty-year machine with hydraulic components, proprietary filters, and increasingly some electronics. Over that period you will need parts, warranty work, and probably a service call for something that cannot be trailered. The quality of your local dealer determines the entire experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you compare specifications, find out which brands have a dealer within a reasonable drive, then visit the parts counter rather than the sales floor. Ask what they stock for the model you are considering, what their turnaround is on a hydraulic hose, and whether they do field service. The answers vary enormously, and they tell you far more about the next fifteen years than a horsepower comparison will.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why Kubota and John Deere command a premium that the specification sheets do not justify, and it is a legitimate reason to pay it. It is equally why Kioti, LS, and Massey Ferguson can be outstanding buys in regions where they have strong dealers — the machines are good, and with local support the value advantage is real. The combination that goes wrong is a value brand with no nearby dealer, and that is worth avoiding regardless of how good the deal looks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tractor maintenance is simple and unforgiving. Engine oil every 200 hours or annually, hydraulic fluid and filters at the interval in the manual — usually 400 to 500 hours — air filter checked far more often than you expect if you mow in dust, and greasing every zerk on the loader and three-point hitch every 10 to 50 hours depending on the point. The greasing is the one owners skip, and worn loader pins are the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diesel fuel deserves attention on a machine that sits. Modern tractors are sensitive to water and microbial growth in the fuel, so keep the tank full over winter to limit condensation, use a stabiliser if the machine will sit for months, and change the fuel filter on schedule rather than when it starts running badly.
          </p>

          <p>
            On safety, three rules cover most of what goes wrong. Keep the rollover protection structure up and the seatbelt on — a ROPS folded down to fit under a barn door and left there is how a survivable rollover becomes fatal. Carry the loader low when travelling, because a raised bucket moves the centre of gravity dramatically. And never lift a heavy load without rear ballast; the point at which the rear wheels get light is the point at which you have lost steering and are close to tipping.
          </p>
        </div>


