              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a backhoe attachment worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have trenching, stump removal, or drainage work ahead of you, yes. A mini excavator rents for around $350 a day plus transport, so three or four project days a year makes a real dent in the cost, and owning one means you do the job when it suits you. If your work is mowing and material handling, skip it and put the money into implements.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which compact tractor brand holds its value best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Kubota, closely followed by John Deere — both commonly retain 60 to 70 percent of purchase price after five years, which substantially lowers real cost of ownership. Kioti, LS, and Massey Ferguson trade at a larger discount used, so their lower purchase price is partly recovered by the market when you sell. Buy a value brand to keep it, not to trade it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need 4WD on a compact tractor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in nearly every case. Four-wheel drive transforms loader performance, because a loaded bucket lifts weight off the rear wheels and a two-wheel-drive machine simply spins. It also matters on any slope, in mud, and for snow work. The premium is a few thousand dollars and it is the last option to cut from a build.
