        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Compact Tractor Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tractors are bought once or twice in a lifetime, usually from a sales floor, and the same handful of errors repeat in every buyer forum.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Horsepower Instead of Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sub-compact and a compact tractor with the same rated horsepower are not comparable machines. Weight is what keeps a box blade cutting and a rotary cutter steady, and it is why a heavier tractor with identical horsepower does work the lighter one cannot. Compare operating weight and hitch category first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Brand Before the Dealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You will own this machine for fifteen or twenty years and you will need parts, filters, and warranty work throughout. A great tractor with the nearest dealer two hours away is a worse purchase than a good tractor with a strong dealer ten minutes away. Visit parts counters before you compare specifications.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Rear Ballast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lifting a full bucket without ballast lightens the rear wheels, which costs steering and can tip the machine forward. A ballast box or loaded rear tires is safety equipment, not an accessory, and it also improves traction on every task. It is the cheapest important thing you will buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Budgeting for the Tractor and Not the Implements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Implements routinely add 30 to 50 percent to the total, and a tractor with nothing to hook to it does one job. Decide on the loader, the ground-engaging implement, and the ballast at the same time you decide on the machine, and negotiate them as one package.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the ROPS Folded Down</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rollover protection is folded to fit under a low door and then left there, and that is how survivable rollovers become fatal. Fold it only when you must, put it back up immediately, and wear the seatbelt whenever the structure is up — the belt is what keeps you inside the protected zone.
              </p>
